[{:db/id "q-127139/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "420a7c0f-5081-5e70-a57b-36c6b2a42651" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127139/selection/9f3c5525e8594cb69619627b8e14363bd3a225d4c6b1fa2067a7b3dd5aab24b5" :answer/id #uuid "6e9063e9-0d07-5d5c-a47b-694f6c83bf33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127139/selection/14a43d3af896bf05563785727a4bb6d0e93fdd15f9c0806d6da072b2ca577574" :answer/id #uuid "1209b344-c94e-55d7-8523-8d1c3e86e047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127139/selection/66f89f02788b769493b40f3413bfd828e35abd9d0de798b984d8655e590acb4c" :answer/id #uuid "69e9e3f9-c370-5885-83c1-8278f41fe9bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127139/selection/48bca1fa3f2d29ebd011cf72abbf4213a1a61062b7135355a585c78528fae460" :answer/id #uuid "42decc75-13a0-52e9-86cf-5b0f426f17f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127139/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5ddc65-7c77-5d3b-96c4-e37f80bac19b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127139/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127139/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127139/selection/9f3c5525e8594cb69619627b8e14363bd3a225d4c6b1fa2067a7b3dd5aab24b5" "q-127139/selection/14a43d3af896bf05563785727a4bb6d0e93fdd15f9c0806d6da072b2ca577574" "q-127139/selection/66f89f02788b769493b40f3413bfd828e35abd9d0de798b984d8655e590acb4c" "q-127139/selection/48bca1fa3f2d29ebd011cf72abbf4213a1a61062b7135355a585c78528fae460"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127139" :question/id #uuid "29696ebe-afdf-551a-9420-1a7c07435361" :question/math-academy-id "q-127139" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=49{cos}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(49{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(1)\\cdot (-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{3}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-98{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}-98{u}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =(98\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127139/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127139"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2589156d-0a88-53bf-9d3d-ff652605751d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060/selection/3875779d954ccd062ffefb0aa5d678d5d954d89ae398437bf57ca25e2989b361" :answer/id #uuid "ca3371ee-c68a-5ea8-8fb9-29d3aa8566be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060/selection/749eff2985363dccc5bb12451aaaed76442e7a824c86bae23bd6cb3d8de20d0d" :answer/id #uuid "f7e0acd7-202f-58dc-9511-05aadd5e3a1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060/selection/8a062f85dfae2453091ecd95389dc9b0751d8bb7c18126361428cf17100dc6b2" :answer/id #uuid "efcfc2b5-b4cb-597a-9e14-b16570d9dde1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060/selection/0195d8fc4e76018af063e63082c6a1512ae939688d3600f4a9d6e5ceaaa5533a" :answer/id #uuid "8d00f716-54f9-5750-bc4d-37042aa8b5a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5976fcb3-aaed-56cc-b56c-e2a8dfaee6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127060/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127060/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127060/selection/3875779d954ccd062ffefb0aa5d678d5d954d89ae398437bf57ca25e2989b361" "q-127060/selection/749eff2985363dccc5bb12451aaaed76442e7a824c86bae23bd6cb3d8de20d0d" "q-127060/selection/8a062f85dfae2453091ecd95389dc9b0751d8bb7c18126361428cf17100dc6b2" "q-127060/selection/0195d8fc4e76018af063e63082c6a1512ae939688d3600f4a9d6e5ceaaa5533a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127060" :question/id #uuid "3cd811a3-e69a-5719-8218-9dc6952b534b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127060" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}-16{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127060/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127060"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077/selection/a48167d8e2245609f108956c9f8c842fb7c6c9f3393048058df50a45ea21b07e" :answer/id #uuid "f89e8695-e13e-58ff-a224-e9760208b3c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-30\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077/selection/939e1b9bcb891fb12637c2e2df92c0092a3f4e138803bc6a726aafcb70c12541" :answer/id #uuid "dd71ce2d-9813-5bfc-97d1-9928c61a3a7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-60\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077/selection/dca43121b3eb4830c84573d0cfb0171a13fe0f2eab9806e0b523724c6707b363" :answer/id #uuid "83ef4fac-ffd1-5716-bbfd-0b58328dfda9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7228b8fc-f983-5250-b346-c322353e0e79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077/selection/81f91315258fab2aa012e47c6a345697f96e593864d9f48b7ac6b6f62e32bdbd" :answer/id #uuid "9afdae31-1a52-55c8-8be8-aff77d6c0f0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f51f65e-114e-5009-af56-01206a983922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127077/selection/81f91315258fab2aa012e47c6a345697f96e593864d9f48b7ac6b6f62e32bdbd" :answer-field/choices ["q-127077/selection/a48167d8e2245609f108956c9f8c842fb7c6c9f3393048058df50a45ea21b07e" "q-127077/selection/939e1b9bcb891fb12637c2e2df92c0092a3f4e138803bc6a726aafcb70c12541" "q-127077/selection/dca43121b3eb4830c84573d0cfb0171a13fe0f2eab9806e0b523724c6707b363" "q-127077/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127077/selection/81f91315258fab2aa012e47c6a345697f96e593864d9f48b7ac6b6f62e32bdbd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127077" :question/id #uuid "e28c839f-0da0-57d1-9a07-c78bdcce70e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-127077" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =60\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127077/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127077"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140/selection/315015d2ca8754d78a117aabc78ea3a80a25b12cbc40b9b4cf9db424d10cd0cf" :answer/id #uuid "ecb57bdb-d8b5-552c-8a2e-0c7403aedfdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "b8500164-a8e2-5fa0-82aa-75b47be295ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140/selection/0416a3b65e75455db4beab74dd083a23f363c016b531da6c156659cc1c82a45c" :answer/id #uuid "cc30d1da-d330-5e15-914d-9538b17387da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "192\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140/selection/0bd5bc39fb27a6790691ca246c162de46d18e417f17d77fcc7fb55dc5411ee80" :answer/id #uuid "c996dd2e-af1c-56bb-b75a-1f5b72423f19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140/selection/710eadc0ed84e84489104e15142d504b743aa0df7ab3d9eb1064fc8c5b0baca5" :answer/id #uuid "8fdb6cef-c15d-5bdb-9454-9c9c2c777c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "384\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "706dd9dd-cab9-59c5-98fe-6b28577c0ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127140/selection/710eadc0ed84e84489104e15142d504b743aa0df7ab3d9eb1064fc8c5b0baca5" :answer-field/choices ["q-127140/selection/315015d2ca8754d78a117aabc78ea3a80a25b12cbc40b9b4cf9db424d10cd0cf" "q-127140/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127140/selection/0416a3b65e75455db4beab74dd083a23f363c016b531da6c156659cc1c82a45c" "q-127140/selection/0bd5bc39fb27a6790691ca246c162de46d18e417f17d77fcc7fb55dc5411ee80" "q-127140/selection/710eadc0ed84e84489104e15142d504b743aa0df7ab3d9eb1064fc8c5b0baca5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127140" :question/id #uuid "6ea2f61d-2898-503f-a0d7-93a797a0d250" :question/math-academy-id "q-127140" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x=4(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4y=4(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =192t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =192(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =384\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127140/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127140"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "f657c978-f275-5f0d-a67c-3949e4a24532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" :answer/id #uuid "0d0349b9-b179-587a-afaa-85295bb5b54b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081/selection/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" :answer/id #uuid "dda69729-c24d-53ff-9c40-b735488c10b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba" :answer/id #uuid "d550f9f7-0e9a-528d-8027-c6f33feb2646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "19269622-70f2-5d77-8e58-805942412c5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b1a69a-63ad-5007-b238-6365d955d9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127081/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127081/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127081/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" "q-127081/selection/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" "q-127081/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba" "q-127081/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127081" :question/id #uuid "2a2afc1b-bf60-516e-9d6d-04a932ea57b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127081" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i+9{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =9{x}^{3}=9{cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=u,du=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(9{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{1}^{1}(u+{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127081/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127081"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" :answer/id #uuid "1e64141b-2b34-5536-8988-238c143305fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080/selection/655610c33f9ee70bd034b89a5aa2c6ed9461fb56dcb4fb9b8027be65bbde5b10" :answer/id #uuid "b0edc086-e4db-520f-a713-61ccfade5452" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080/selection/181f69e2ddda97cdc36f295b113d0a8a7a6e23842533f39b3be526bc60f94b57" :answer/id #uuid "bd1f43bb-75b5-51ce-b47d-c5c53b05463d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "6e22846a-b285-5d40-8ae7-d3e4e3e8064e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88" :answer/id #uuid "8e55415f-b5a3-5a8b-97cf-2b54c5018931" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0afa333-a50f-521b-ae4f-9019720c3be8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127080/selection/655610c33f9ee70bd034b89a5aa2c6ed9461fb56dcb4fb9b8027be65bbde5b10" :answer-field/choices ["q-127080/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" "q-127080/selection/655610c33f9ee70bd034b89a5aa2c6ed9461fb56dcb4fb9b8027be65bbde5b10" "q-127080/selection/181f69e2ddda97cdc36f295b113d0a8a7a6e23842533f39b3be526bc60f94b57" "q-127080/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127080/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127080" :question/id #uuid "eac681a3-ee24-5aa0-8779-00b62c1b9a7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127080" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-16y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $4$ and $1,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x-16y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127080/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127080"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079/selection/be6820722cd1d35c917e2f43aeab2943c0edea480231fe3c1573045a9ecc2867" :answer/id #uuid "2e33fdcd-2744-56bb-b52b-9d74d824fba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-48\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "758279ae-1910-531f-87af-bc9a4dc37fa1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079/selection/315015d2ca8754d78a117aabc78ea3a80a25b12cbc40b9b4cf9db424d10cd0cf" :answer/id #uuid "81976287-3d1c-5ffa-8634-11221c093597" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079/selection/b53e7bad260d7329b1383e38084a7ff31160df9790db03487b5a85c38e587e88" :answer/id #uuid "d0525412-a7e7-5d27-96f7-24b19bd93dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-24\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079/selection/c6fda912a8972e4c6fd403a23a2c7597afb73ea7a67811f27707166340db5e2f" :answer/id #uuid "7a71f602-30e0-527c-a5d8-211d627bac06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "34b8990b-4e8b-5e7a-b0d1-a90b0d9e60c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127079/selection/c6fda912a8972e4c6fd403a23a2c7597afb73ea7a67811f27707166340db5e2f" :answer-field/choices ["q-127079/selection/be6820722cd1d35c917e2f43aeab2943c0edea480231fe3c1573045a9ecc2867" "q-127079/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127079/selection/315015d2ca8754d78a117aabc78ea3a80a25b12cbc40b9b4cf9db424d10cd0cf" "q-127079/selection/b53e7bad260d7329b1383e38084a7ff31160df9790db03487b5a85c38e587e88" "q-127079/selection/c6fda912a8972e4c6fd403a23a2c7597afb73ea7a67811f27707166340db5e2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127079" :question/id #uuid "47de8e96-8132-5dda-895d-23ad59f9be01" :question/math-academy-id "q-127079" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3x\\,i+3y\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =3y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }24{cos}^{2}⁡t+24{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }24\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =24t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =24(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =48\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127079/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127079"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "aeb1a41e-2532-5b6e-9c2e-afe361b983cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "e94b1e07-f431-523f-9965-d502316cf5db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "d6baf4a7-03be-58ad-9fa9-2d59d6dc5a03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "3ce049d8-617a-5ad9-afc3-a4b08207e255" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "86e62f91-bb49-5c00-89e2-42eeebe6f8d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "199de684-df9a-5e52-96da-33a9c2a2c613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127143/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127143/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-127143/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127143/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-127143/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-127143/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127143" :question/id #uuid "ff4fdda4-929e-5d0d-bc3e-784da58a139c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127143" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $1.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}={sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{3}={cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({sin}^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127143/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127143"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144/selection/01bbf8ebd5069470431f8452e98a426e151975cb8793ee4f306144a87060a9a3" :answer/id #uuid "83566363-d32e-58c4-9f21-6695086c5902" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144/selection/77e5f609e5d8d491060e3a10614d0baf017b9dbf04da6561c7e9cb8462621053" :answer/id #uuid "eae3cd37-c45b-5b2c-9c7b-e234e516ad8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "6c35fc07-ccce-5789-a66f-c1f15bb7f9db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144/selection/7c07cb852d1d9d8f5c291070fae09ae7d988365e5d8d9e30f8cf9e144690b3ce" :answer/id #uuid "24b3841e-3a58-559d-9f2b-9d1b371fc645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144/selection/fcc327425bffa26181c03d4b5128695b2377c88b52a7f5521a7198aa3c9e78a0" :answer/id #uuid "e50dcb29-522d-5bb0-b751-6a2428e82f63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "37686ea6-ae29-5495-8729-e3508c7301c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127144/selection/77e5f609e5d8d491060e3a10614d0baf017b9dbf04da6561c7e9cb8462621053" :answer-field/choices ["q-127144/selection/01bbf8ebd5069470431f8452e98a426e151975cb8793ee4f306144a87060a9a3" "q-127144/selection/77e5f609e5d8d491060e3a10614d0baf017b9dbf04da6561c7e9cb8462621053" "q-127144/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127144/selection/7c07cb852d1d9d8f5c291070fae09ae7d988365e5d8d9e30f8cf9e144690b3ce" "q-127144/selection/fcc327425bffa26181c03d4b5128695b2377c88b52a7f5521a7198aa3c9e78a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127144" :question/id #uuid "10a5efd5-d2d2-5ab6-89ba-62ed4942cb46" :question/math-academy-id "q-127144" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-(x+y)\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $1.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127144/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145/selection/66f89f02788b769493b40f3413bfd828e35abd9d0de798b984d8655e590acb4c" :answer/id #uuid "e1ba467a-9da9-5198-bb20-105670a5b35e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145/selection/14a43d3af896bf05563785727a4bb6d0e93fdd15f9c0806d6da072b2ca577574" :answer/id #uuid "d143f9ca-cfdc-52a1-88ef-3ca911a71c7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145/selection/9f3c5525e8594cb69619627b8e14363bd3a225d4c6b1fa2067a7b3dd5aab24b5" :answer/id #uuid "1bd7e956-c4df-5113-9e6d-18022a206343" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145/selection/b190992088af31bb631456634391eb1223389b7dd9ec1d9a83a1c368d79ec16f" :answer/id #uuid "06bb92da-9b86-508d-9134-62cfe0a97f61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "731bde59-f11a-53cd-a88e-23c8232e0d70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6610bfb8-5fcc-5cba-ae06-4a8a099de7dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127145/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-127145/selection/66f89f02788b769493b40f3413bfd828e35abd9d0de798b984d8655e590acb4c" "q-127145/selection/14a43d3af896bf05563785727a4bb6d0e93fdd15f9c0806d6da072b2ca577574" "q-127145/selection/9f3c5525e8594cb69619627b8e14363bd3a225d4c6b1fa2067a7b3dd5aab24b5" "q-127145/selection/b190992088af31bb631456634391eb1223389b7dd9ec1d9a83a1c368d79ec16f" "q-127145/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127145" :question/id #uuid "93a1a02e-eb1c-56b6-9bba-93fa74522619" :question/math-academy-id "q-127145" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-2x\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $4.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2x=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127145/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127145"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "8631ca9a-23d9-5c00-a0ee-e16878bb00cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153/selection/f5fd5e92ead50ffb0d1b470a1bcc42bc9e20b6cd404726b178caca95f2eb4406" :answer/id #uuid "e0798de1-6b8e-5d0e-88f4-5143d920a64c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "7c4bde79-54e7-57d2-9123-a59ef75df2b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "bc8327db-44a7-5427-af53-9145f8a7b0b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153/selection/268574f80940018f334184d7d194bc0ec7b107e7fb864dd82012507293979e1b" :answer/id #uuid "8329b4b7-6b69-5cf9-982f-f97724d72d96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "99417d4a-769e-5f0e-9aa2-bb671b7bf87d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127153/selection/268574f80940018f334184d7d194bc0ec7b107e7fb864dd82012507293979e1b" :answer-field/choices ["q-127153/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-127153/selection/f5fd5e92ead50ffb0d1b470a1bcc42bc9e20b6cd404726b178caca95f2eb4406" "q-127153/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-127153/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127153/selection/268574f80940018f334184d7d194bc0ec7b107e7fb864dd82012507293979e1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127153" :question/id #uuid "bbd6b7d5-dad7-5c0e-bd3a-49f70eaa3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-127153" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/fbfe241043e9014c1b633d163cf7e06234db70702a9052297b5df8b9df3fd82f.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/d432857e3350dfcb70a2a00b66ad9b86bc207382edd277c2169be0834c1710e0.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}\\frac{1}{t}+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127153/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127153"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "8a5d1daa-477b-511a-ae3e-ea913ab2ae33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "45682f7a-c1b3-57c6-aaa0-83442d081dd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "64ddfc15-6918-5149-8bca-7bf4fa826f07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "94c30da2-b983-56c5-8c00-6a236ac61a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1c721d56-fdac-5d8f-b2f8-c14e0cc3faf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8280d8f2-2dfa-5e27-804b-f7f144641793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127253/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-127253/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-127253/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-127253/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-127253/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127253/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127253" :question/id #uuid "2ea662d2-7a30-55b0-b0d0-0769f25e46a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127253" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/e288ed1095be61e4645fb63c22b7fd2cb605d34795c30557e84afb31b7ec8721.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1)\\cdot (-1)-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-127253/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127253"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-126943"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\,i-4\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2.)$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/b7ac1ce4a79528f5660b8f898739da72489ebe8e8fc156020efae77a103fbec4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2)\\cdot (1)-(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "173e1610-4ad0-547d-bbbe-103fa3589594" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "21208418-7f88-50d3-a8b5-f0ef5cc29b20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba" :answer/id #uuid "4dff37d2-cda6-5d07-8c82-df728b2f6607" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" :answer/id #uuid "3d42d32f-d121-5cc9-bb06-3640cd7e9033" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "8437b2a9-a3d3-5950-a00b-a92f10bb4d96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e635918e-30da-5590-8a31-9f0712131189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127223/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" :answer-field/choices ["q-127223/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-127223/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127223/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba" "q-127223/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" "q-127223/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127223" :question/id #uuid "7bf838a7-e1a6-51ee-80bb-2a2ce8710507" :question/math-academy-id "q-127223" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/414611a90c2227ff163d40a4f6f560bc67c471fa0f35724e459cec280e4904f9.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4\\,i+2\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/11376e6ef18b151a6dd1a02dd09f20b72bce3160c718f4095b4f08b23721ff1f.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2t})=-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(4)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}})-(2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{t}-t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)-(4-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127223/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127223"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "2b1d3741-52c0-5bfd-89f6-738fd959e621" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "9108f1b6-bed0-533e-91f9-7a042c91334d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "bd11cbad-dbb5-5e41-b4c0-f6b9a79528d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "cad5bf5f-e3ed-5105-8a11-9eb6f4b766ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "fb19e00b-d8a1-5d28-8910-c12e82cbeefd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "69e1a391-f34a-5ba7-9a6d-24a9629e9a2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127233/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-127233/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-127233/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-127233/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127233/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-127233/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127233" :question/id #uuid "212d69ff-5a94-5a5a-bac7-381f6573a066" :question/math-academy-id "q-127233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/9bb15934c271a17851342b9032f5096f48e7ac9ee4e149af92ac1010dea46d88.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i+(2x+1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/0e751adb9f44d24ba89af318fe5f063b1be146bbae45d05fc2dd9a99bef259f2.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3xy=3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x+1=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2})(1)-(2t+1)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2t-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1-1)-(0-0-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127233/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127233"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9560"] :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9561"] :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9574"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
